WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:00.940
SAAD ALI: Hi, everyone.

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:01.680
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.810
I'm a software engineer
here at Google at KubeCon.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.693
And here with me now is
Louis, an engineer on Istio.

00:00:06.693 --> 00:00:08.109
Louis, can you
introduce yourself?

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:09.400
LOUIS RYAN: Hi, I'm Louis Ryan.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.140
I work on Istio for Google--

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.690
I've been working on it since
the start of the project,

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.481
and just really excited
to be here and talk

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:15.330
about the project.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.910
SAAD ALI: So we've been
hearing a lot about Istio.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.700
What is it and how does it fit
into the KubeCon and Kubernetes

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.930
world?

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.690
LOUIS RYAN: So Istio is what
we call a service mesh, which

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.940
is a very abstract definition.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.310
But what really Istio
does is it gives you

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.960
a way to kind of elevate
the networking concepts,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.980
and allow you to manage how
services, micro-services, VMs,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.810
and all these different
types of workloads

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.750
communicate with each other, and
apply policies to those things,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.434
and extract telemetry
from that traffic.

00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:47.100
So you get a much
deeper set of insights

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.770
about how your
network is behaving,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.480
how your applications
are behaving,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.880
and use that to drive
operational practices,

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.300
and improve the security
posture of your deployments.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.020
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.260
So it sounds like before
Istio, folks were basically

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.690
building custom logic into
every single application

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:08.550
to do things like logging
and network routing.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.040
And with Istio, all of
that gets extracted out

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.962
of the applications in
one single control plain.

00:01:13.962 --> 00:01:14.670
LOUIS RYAN: Yeah.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.820
So the goal is to kind of
unburden application developers

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.899
from all these common
concerns that, depending

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:23.190
on how many platforms you
are building your application

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.400
code in, you would see a
high degree of variability

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.690
in the completeness or the
quality of those integrations

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.710
although they're pieces
of the infrastructure.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.950
And so Istio hopes
to kind of give

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:39.030
a high, uniform base of
those type of capabilities

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.460
out of the box to
infrastructural operators.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.520
So ideally , application
developers have to think a lot

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.830
less about those concerns,
and by definition,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.950
their application code becomes
simpler over time by doing so.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.440
And so it creates this
nice separation of concerns

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.462
in operations for companies.

00:01:54.462 --> 00:01:55.170
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.610
And is Istio
something that folks

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.992
should start with from the
beginning, or something they

00:01:58.992 --> 00:02:00.450
should consider
later down the line

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.087
as they begin to get larger?

00:02:03.087 --> 00:02:04.920
LOUIS RYAN: So we see
both at the same time,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.060
almost by definition.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.530
There are obviously
lots and lots

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.760
of really, really large
deployments out there,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.450
but we also see
companies adopting

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.744
Istio from the beginning
just because people recognize

00:02:15.744 --> 00:02:17.910
the value it has in their
development and operations

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.061
practices.

00:02:19.061 --> 00:02:21.060
So we kind of see these
two different deployment

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.860
modalities.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.830
When they adopt Greenfield--

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.460
and it's just very
natural, and they

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.251
don't have to go through
upgrade processes.

00:02:28.251 --> 00:02:31.140
But Istio still has to be
able to be taken on-board

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.034
by a large deployment and
incrementally rolled out,

00:02:34.034 --> 00:02:36.450
so we make sure we have features
that are there to support

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.142
those types of deployments.

00:02:38.142 --> 00:02:38.850
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.220
And what is the current
status of Istio?

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:45.120
LOUIS RYAN: So Istio, we went
1.0 in the summer of last year,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.900
and we've been kind of
pushing, like any project,

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.120
to patch releases to support
stability, and reliability,

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.747
and scale.

00:02:52.747 --> 00:02:55.080
The next major release we
have coming is in the end of--

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.420
sometime in January--
the 1.1 release.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.420
There'll be some major
kind of performance

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.330
and architectural
improvements to the product.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:06.870
So yeah, like any project,
it's moving forward,

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.630
we're adding features,
it's scaling up,

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:10.770
we're onboarding customers.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.810
And it's just moving along.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.570
SAAD ALI: And I heard recently
that GKE announced beta support

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:16.140
for Istio.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.950
Can you talk a little
bit about that?

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:18.658
LOUIS RYAN: Yeah.

00:03:18.658 --> 00:03:20.410
So what-- we've been
working on this.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.180
We kind of announced
it at Next last year,

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:23.721
and it went through
an alpha program.

00:03:23.721 --> 00:03:26.040
And we just had the
beta announcement--

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.910
just providing a
nice kind of unified,

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.610
out-of-the-box installation and
management experience around

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.470
Istio to make it easy for GKE
customers to start using its

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:35.654
features.

00:03:35.654 --> 00:03:38.070
We're really excited to get
that in the hands of customers

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:43.740
and get that feedback, because
Istio and open source projects,

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.320
without some help in install,
you have to do a bit of work.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:50.790
And so on GCP itself,
we can take away

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.997
some of that installation
headache for people

00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:54.580
and just make their
experience easier.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.270
SAAD ALI: So what parts
of Istio does GKE manage?

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.700
Is it the deployment only
or does it go beyond that?

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.300
LOUIS RYAN: So currently, it's
the install and the upgrade.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.130
And particularly, the
upgrade is important.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.200
We will make sure that
the system automatically

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.500
upgrades as we add new
capabilities and new releases.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:15.034
Over time, we'll make the
system even more managed.

00:04:15.034 --> 00:04:17.700
That will include the ability to
scale up the amount of traffic,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.991
and the number of pods, and
workloads, and other things

00:04:19.991 --> 00:04:22.860
that GKE handles, and handle
multi-cluster deployments

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.860
and other more
complex topologies.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:27.740
SAAD ALI: And in
terms of open source,

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.910
I know Istio has a
lot of contributions

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.330
from multiple companies
and independent folks.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.072
Can you talk about the community
and what's going on there?

00:04:36.072 --> 00:04:36.780
LOUIS RYAN: Yeah.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.290
So Istio was born as
a community effort,

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:45.570
and so you see not just Google,
but IBM, Red Hat, Pivotal.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.270
We use the Envoy Proxy from
Lyft, which obviously, Google

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.741
is also heavily invested in.

00:04:50.741 --> 00:04:52.240
So there's a really
large community.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.300
I've been wandering around
talking to people at the booths

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.140
here, and finding out
about integrations

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.390
they've written with Istio
that I didn't know existed.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:01.070
So it's great to see the
kind of organic groundswell

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:03.322
of community support
around the project.

00:05:03.322 --> 00:05:05.870
But it's very
community-driven, we

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.550
run very much like
Kubernetes does

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.560
in our processes
and our governance.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.101
So it's an open project.

00:05:12.101 --> 00:05:13.850
We're proud of the
fact that we're helping

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.275
to push open source forward.

00:05:15.275 --> 00:05:16.250
SAAD ALI: Well,
it sounds awesome.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.380
Let's shift gears a little
bit and talk about KubeCon.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:19.671
This is not your first KubeCon.

00:05:19.671 --> 00:05:22.000
LOUIS RYAN: This is
not my first KubeCon,

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.800
though each KubeCon does seem
to get exponentially larger.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.724
So it's amazing--

00:05:29.724 --> 00:05:31.640
I always like to go
around and talk to vendors

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.580
and see what they're
doing, and it's just

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.902
amazing to see how much
traction Kubernetes is getting,

00:05:37.902 --> 00:05:39.860
and all the integrations
that that platform has

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:41.471
driven in the ecosystem.

00:05:41.471 --> 00:05:43.470
But now I'm talking to
vendors about this Istio,

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:44.930
and so that's kind
of exciting for me.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:45.020
SAAD ALI: Yeah.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.610
I think you mentioned
that you haven't

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.170
had a chance to go to
any of the talks because

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:49.850
of the number of people that
are interested in talking

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:50.870
to you about Istio.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:52.245
LOUIS RYAN: Yeah,
this was always

00:05:52.245 --> 00:05:54.600
going to be kind of the
hallway track for me this year.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.350
I'm here to represent Istio
and to talk about Google

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.110
and Google's use, and how
we're pushing the service mesh

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.840
story forward, so yes--
not a lot of talks,

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.790
though, I'm talking
tomorrow, so I at least

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:06.200
get to attend my own session.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:07.670
SAAD ALI: What is that about?

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.400
LOUIS RYAN: So it's a
panel just discussing

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.381
where service mesh is,
what factors and forces

00:06:13.381 --> 00:06:15.380
in the industry are driving
it, and where we all

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:16.088
think it's going.

00:06:16.088 --> 00:06:19.420
And so there'll be some
folks from Linkerd talking

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.170
about their service
mesh products,

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:24.969
some folks from IBM, me,
and Zack from [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:24.969 --> 00:06:25.760
will also be there.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.230
SAAD ALI: Sounds like it will
be a great session for folks

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:28.710
interested in Istio then.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:29.418
LOUIS RYAN: Yeah.

00:06:29.418 --> 00:06:31.495
It's tomorrow around
11:40, I think.

00:06:31.495 --> 00:06:32.120
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:33.590
Keep an eye on the schedule.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:37.070
And finally, what's your
favorite KubeCon memory?

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.680
LOUIS RYAN: So while this
is not my first KubeCon,

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.070
the very first KubeCon
I went to is actually

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.976
the inception moment for Istio.

00:06:45.976 --> 00:06:48.350
We were thinking internally
about building an open source

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:50.750
service mesh product, and we
were looking at technologies

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.650
in the industry, and we had
found this kind of side project

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.904
that IBM was building called--

00:06:56.904 --> 00:06:58.820
oh, I'm trying to remember
the name of it now.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:00.260
[LAUGHING]

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.674
But anyway, we met the
project lead for that here,

00:07:02.674 --> 00:07:04.340
and we just kind of
started spit balling

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:08.270
about creating this community
effort and rolling up together.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.160
And within a week
of that meeting,

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.118
we had gotten the Istio
project off the ground.

00:07:12.118 --> 00:07:13.970
So I have fond
memories of KubeCon

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.170
because that's where
Istio was born.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.780
SAAD ALI: Istio was
born at KubeCon.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:18.450
That is awesome.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:19.710
Well, it was a pleasure
talking to you.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:20.470
Thank you very much.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:20.940
LOUIS RYAN: Yeah, it was great.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:21.660
Thanks for having me.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:22.350
SAAD ALI: And enjoy the
rest of your KubeCon.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:22.680
LOUIS RYAN: All right.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:23.513
Thank you very much.

00:07:23.513 --> 00:07:26.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

